comments.body:
On a closer look, I'm wondering whether we really need `Condition` to be an interface, or if we can just make it the following:
```
type Condition struct {
  ID string
  Type string
  LazyGetCondition func() string
  LazyGetDescription func() string
  EvaluateFunc func(ctx context.Context, data ConditionsData) ConditionEvaluationResult
}
func (c Condition) Condition() string {
  if c.LazyGetCondition != nil {
    return c.LazyGetCondition()
  }
  return ""
}
func (c Condition) Description() string {
  if c.LazyGetDescription != nil {
    return c.LazyGetDescription()
  }
  return ""
}
func (c Condition) Evaluate(ctx context.Context, data ConditionsData) ConditionEvaluationResult {
  if c.EvaluateFunc != nil {
    return c.EvaluteFunc(ctx, data)
  }
  return ConditionEvaluationResultUnevaluatable()
}
```

(Condition/Description only lazy like the following if you want, I'd opt for just making them static strings for now.)

This preserves the "node authorizer can evaluate its conditions fast in-memory", as the node authorizer could still populate `EvaluateFunc` on the condition returned, and just implement its `EvaluateConditions` as `conditionsMap.Evaluate(ctx, data, nil)`, without needing any lookup table or even setting the `Condition` field on the conditions.

This would remove the complications around checking for the condition being nil, or trying to deepcopy conditions. Would that be a fair thing to do?

@liggitt I addressed the final outstanding comments in the last five commits.

Let me know when you've reviewed that diff, and I'll squash all changes into 4-5 logical commits.

A couple of notes:

1. I noted that it doesn't really make sense for conditional decisions to return a non-nil `Error` or non-empty `Reason`, so I don't implement those for union either (which I initially in this PR did)
1. And as discussed on Slack; right now, no incomplete error list is returned when a condition evaluates to true and the process short-circuits. They are anyways not returned as errors for the Deny or NoOpinion case, and an incomplete list for the special case of allow seems more harmful than helpful until we figure out the warnings for the Deny and NoOpinion cases.
1. I at the end of the day chose to flatten the structure for `ConditionsData` to avoid the nesting, as I remembered that we already made the return value `(Decision, string, error)` and for it to make sense to take in other inputs (which would have partial information), the return value would need to be `ConditionsAwareDecision`, so it's better that we add an explicit method with dedicated partial support if we want it e.g. for authenticator-provided attenuation or constrained impersonation later.


